Calico and Ciliuim:
CNI: Container Network Interface: are a set of standards on how programs must be developed to solve networking challenges in a container runtime environment. Here programs
equals to plugins. CNI will tell how plugins must be developed and how Container Runtime shall invoke them.
CNI sets a set of responsibilities to Container Runtime and Plugins.
For Container Runtime:
It is responsible to create a network namespace for each container.
It shall Identify the networks that container must attach to. 
And Container Runtime must invoke the plugin(BRIDGE) when the container is created using the ADD command. 
DEL command to invoke the plugin(BRIDGE) when the container is deleted. 
How to configure a Network configuration in JSON Format
For Plugins:
Must support command line arguments ADD/DEL/CHECK
Must support parameters container ID, Network NS etc.
Must Manage IP Address assignment to PODS
Must Return results in a specific format. 
Calico and Cilium are third party plugins that adhere to the CNI standards and thus can be invoked by container runtime. 
Calico: Open source networking and network security solution for containerized environments like k8s. 
Features:
BGP Based Networking: Border Gateway Protocol for dynamic routing. 
Network Policy: enforces NP to control and secure traffic b/w pods
Scalability: suitable for large K8s clusters
Interoperability: suitable for multi-cloud and hybrid cloud scenarios

Cilium: Open source networking and security plugin for enhanced observability and security and network connectivity.
Features:
Uses eBPF technology for fine grained observability and control.
API aware network security policies which allows us to fine grain control over application traffic.
Transparent Encryption: It provides encryption b/w pods and nodes without requiring application changes. 
Layer 7 Proxy: for handling HTTP and gRPC traffic
